      "review_issues": []
    },
    {
      "id": "p0110-b004",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "After consulting with Toby, the translator settled on oira for Sans' pronoun. In everyday Japanese, oira gives off a \"rural, unsophisticated bumpkin\" vibe, but in entertainment it's also used by aloof, happy-go-lucky characters. It's important to note that this choice clashed with the existing fan-made translations, in which Sans used the more assertive pronoun ore.",
        "raw": "After consulting with Toby, the translator settled on oira for Sans' pronoun.\nIn everyday Japanese, oira gives off a \"rural, unsophisticated bumpkin\" vibe,\nbut in entertainment it's also used by aloof, happy-go-lucky characters.\nIt's important to note that this choice clashed with the existing fan-made\ntranslations, in which Sans used the more assertive pronoun ore.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.29695122,
          0.30732143,
          0.88052846,
          0.39292857
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
          0.2949187,
          0.30660714,
          0.90072154,
          0.394
        ],
        "line_ids": [
          11,
          12,
          13,
          14,
          15
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "text": "与 Toby 商量后，译者选择让 Sans 用 oira 自称。在日常日语中，oira 带有“来自乡下、不谙世故的乡巴佬”的感觉，但娱乐作品里也会让超然、随和的角色使用它。这与已有的粉丝翻译产生了冲突：那些版本让 Sans 用语气更强势的 ore（我）。",
        "status": "draft",
        "spans": [
          {
            "start": 24,
            "end": 28,
            "italic": true,
            "lang": "en"
          },
          {
            "start": 39,
